<section xml:id="sec-section-BP" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Basic Probability Concepts</title>

  <p> 
    Write intro to homework set.....  Goal, Purpose, Instructions....
  </p>


 <exercises xml:id="hw-basic-probability-concepts" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>
   <introduction>
     <p>
       Write intro to interactive homework set.....
     </p>
   </introduction>

   <exercise label="BP-3children">
      <title>Three Children</title>
       <introduction>
         <p>
           Suppose that a couple has three children. We are concerned with the sex of the children at birth. 
         </p>
        </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
           <p>
             Which option best describes the sample space here?
           </p>
        </statement>

    <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m> \{BBB, GBB, BGB, BBG, GGB, GBG, BGG, GGG\} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! This set provides a list of all possible outcomes.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m> \{BBB, GGG, BBG, GGB\} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <feedback>
          <p>
            This option does not take the order of the births into account. 
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m> \{B, G\} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <feedback>
          <p>
            This would be the sample space for the birth of a SINGLE child, not three children.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m> \{B, G, BG, GB, BBB, GBB, BGB, BBG, GGB, GBG, BGG, GGG\} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <feedback>
          <p>
            This would be the sample space if the couple were to have up to three children, not exactly three children.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

   </task>

   <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
 
   <task>
   
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the probability that the second child born will be female. Write your answer as a decimal, accurate to three
        decimal places.
      </p>

      <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.500"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Four out of 8 outcomes in the sample space involve the second child being female.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      
        <test>
        <numcmp min="1.001" max="100"/>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Probabilities can not be larger than 1.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
   </task>
   

   <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

   <task>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the probability that <em>at least</em> the second child will be female. Write your answer as a decimal, accurate to three decimal places.
      </p>

      <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.500"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Four out of 8 outcomes in the sample space involve the second child and possibly another child being female.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0.375"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Close! Reread the prompt to make sure you are computing the desired probability.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>

        <test>
          <numcmp min="1.001" max="100"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Probabilities can not be larger than 1.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    </task>
   </exercise>

  


 <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
 <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->


   
   <exercise label="BP-blood-type">
     <title>Blood Type</title>

     <introduction>
       <p>
         An experiment consists of randomly selecting a person and recording their blood type, which could be A, B, AB, or O, and the status of their Rhesus (Rh) factor, which is either present (+) or not (-).
       </p>
     </introduction>

<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      List the sample space for this experiment by dragging relevant outcomes to the right.
    </p>
  </statement>

     <feedback>
       <p>
         Add later.
       </p>
     </feedback>

     <cardsort>
       <match>
         <premise order="3">A+</premise>
         <premise order="3">A-</premise>
         <premise order="3">B-</premise>
         <premise order="3">B+</premise>
         <premise order="3">AB-</premise>
         <premise order="3">AB+</premise>
         <premise order="3">0-</premise>
         <premise order="3">0+</premise>
         <response>Sample Space { ... }</response>
       </match>
       <match>
         <premise order="2">(+)</premise>
         <premise order="2">(-)</premise>
         <premise order="1">A</premise>
         <premise order="1">B</premise>
         <premise order="1">AB</premise>
         <premise order="1">O</premise>
       </match>
     </cardsort>
   </task>

   <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

   <task>

     <statement correct="no">
       <p>
         All outcomes in the sample space equally likely.
       </p>
     </statement>

     <feedback>
       <p>
         Are some blood types less common than others?
       </p>
     </feedback>
     </task>
   </exercise>




<exercise label="ex-probability-streaming-experiment" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Probability Theory: Sequential Sample Spaces</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An undecided college student wants to watch something online but cannot decide how to proceed. They first toss a fair coin to choose a streaming platform: if it lands heads (<m>H</m>), they log into Netflix; if it lands tails (<m>T</m>), they log into Hulu. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Once logged in, they randomly select a piece of content:
            <ul>
                <li><p>If they are on Netflix, they choose randomly between a Movie (<m>M</m>) or a documentary Video (<m>V</m>).</p></li>
                <li><p>If they are on Hulu, they choose randomly between a Comedy show (<m>C</m>), an Anime series (<m>A</m>), or a Reality TV episode (<m>R</m>).</p></li>
            </ul>
            The "experiment" is to observe the combination of the platform selection and the specific content type they choose to watch.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-streaming-space-id">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which of the following sets correctly and comprehensively lists the complete sample space <m>S</m> for this compound experimental sequence?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><m>S = \{(H, M), (H, V), (T, C), (T, A)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This subset leaves out the outcome <m>(T, R)</m> for watching a Reality TV episode on Hulu. A complete sample space must list every possible outcome.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><m>S = \{(H, M), (H, V), (H, C), (H, A), (H, R), (T, M), (T, V), (T, C), (T, A), (T, R)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This list includes combinations that are physically impossible based on the rules of the experiment. For example, they cannot watch a Comedy (<m>C</m>) if they landed on Heads (<m>H</m>) and logged into Netflix.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><m>S = \{(H, M), (H, V), (T, C), (T, A), (T, R)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! The sample space must accurately capture the conditional nature of the choices. If they land on heads (<m>H</m>), only <m>M</m> and <m>V</m> are possible. If they land on tails (<m>T</m>), only <m>C</m>, <m>A</m>, and <m>R</m> are possible. This set contains exactly all 5 valid, mutually exclusive outcomes.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><m>S = \{H, T, M, V, C, A, R\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is a flat list of individual choices rather than an tracking space of ordered outcome pairs representing the entire multi-stage experiment execution loop.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><m>S = \{(H, T), (M, V), (C, A, R)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is just a grouping of the category parameters rather than mapping out the combined sequential actions of the student.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-streaming-event-subset">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <strong>Part B:</strong> Let <m>E</m> denote the compound event that the student chooses to watch a scripted narrative project (defined here as a Movie, a Comedy show, or an Anime series). Which subset correctly defines the event space <m>E</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E = \{M, C, A\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This list contains only the raw content tags. In a compound sample space, elements of an event subset must be written as the full ordered pairs mapped in Part A.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>E = \{(H, M), (T, C), (T, A)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! The subset <m>E</m> filters the total sample space <m>S</m> to capture only the elements matching our narrative constraint: a Movie on Netflix <m>(H, M)</m>, a Comedy on Hulu <m>(T, C)</m>, or an Anime on Hulu <m>(T, A)</m>. It excludes the unscripted options (<m>(H, V)</m> and <m>(T, R)</m>).</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E = \{(H, M), (H, V), (T, C), (T, A), (T, R)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This subset is identical to the entire sample space <m>S</m>. It fails to filter out the unscripted categories (documentary videos <m>V</m> and reality television <m>R</m>).</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E = \{(T, C), (T, A)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This subset is missing the Netflix Movie outcome <m>(H, M)</m>, which is a valid scripted narrative element of this challenge experiment.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E = \{(H, V), (T, R)\}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This subset captures the exact opposite condition, defining the event that the student watches an <em>unscripted</em> project (the complement event, <m>E^c</m>).</p>
                </feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
<task xml:id="task-streaming-likelihood-mc">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <strong>Part C:</strong> Are the five outcomes in the sample space <m>S</m> equally likely to happen? Why or why not?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>Yes, because there are 5 final outcomes, so each outcome automatically has a probability of exactly <m>\frac{1}{5} = 0.20</m>.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. Do not assume a sample space is equally likely just because it is finite. The multi-stage branch structures split the initial coin probabilities into different numbers of subsequent choices.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>No, because the coin probability of 0.50 is split evenly among 2 options on Netflix but split evenly among 3 options on Hulu.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! We find individual outcome probabilities by multiplying along the decision branches. Landing on Netflix (<m>H</m>) has a probability of <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, which is split between 2 choices, making <m>P(H, M) = P(H, V) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{4} = 0.25</m>. Landing on Hulu (<m>T</m>) also has a probability of <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, but it is split among 3 choices, making <m>P(T, C) = P(T, A) = P(T, R) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{6} \approx 0.1667</m>. Because <m>0.25 \neq 0.1667</m>, the outcomes are not equally likely.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>Yes, because the fair coin split guarantees that the conditional options cancel each other out during the second stage selection process.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. The branching fractions do not cancel out; they scale down the probability weight. A choice on Netflix inherits a heavier mathematical weight than a choice on Hulu because it belongs to a smaller final pool.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>No, because the student prefers Netflix over Hulu, which means the coin flip parameters are unweighted and independent.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. The problem explicitly defines the initial stage as a coin flip and states choices are made randomly at each step. Personal preference is not modeled in this experiment.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>Yes, because any experiment tracking a finite sequence of independent Bernoulli events yields flat distribution profiles.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is not a sequence of identical Bernoulli trials (such as flipping the same coin multiple times), but rather a dependent step progression where the second pool size depends on the first outcome.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>


  </exercises>


